module bindings.php.public_route_session_user_install;

// Nextcloud's OCP\IUserSession is the request-scoped authentication state:
// setUser(IUser) installs a user as the active principal and writes that
// identity into the PHP session, isLoggedIn() reports whether the session
// already holds one, and getUser() returns it. The documented way to act as
// a user resolved from an identifier is exactly this pair --
// IUserManager::get($uid) then IUserSession::setUser($user) -- and whether
// that is safe depends entirely on where the identifier came from. On a
// console command the identifier is operator input and the process exits
// with the request; on a route published with @PublicPage the Nextcloud app
// framework dispatches the call without an authenticated session, so the
// identifier is client-supplied and the installed identity is persisted
// into session storage that the next request inherits. Installing a session
// user there without consulting isLoggedIn()/getUser() first both hijacks
// an already-authenticated session and leaves the installed user in place
// after the request, which is the CWE-613 shape: the session is never
// cleared at the boundary where the identity was assumed.
//
// The clauses are class-scoped because that is where the type evidence
// lives -- a PHP call site carries no receiver type, so the binding ties
// the setUser call to the IUserSession dependency two ways: the class
// declares IUserSession (identifier: token from the property or constructor
// parameter, measured confined to the class on the motivating file), and
// the call path's receiver segment is the conventional field name
// (userSession.setUser). The @PublicPage requirement is what separates the
// public route from the console command, and the negation on isLoggedIn is
// the already-authenticated consultation; both were measured load-bearing
// on real collateral -- dropping @PublicPage reports the three
// files_external occ commands, and widening the call match from the
// receiver-qualified path to the bare method name reports two controllers
// whose ->setUser( is INotification::setUser on a notification builder.
//
// Residuals, both stated. The receiver segment is a naming convention: 237
// of 252 IUserSession declarations across the corpus spell it $userSession
// and 15 spell it $session, so a class that names the dependency $session
// is not reported. And the @PublicPage clause reads the class body text,
// so a route made public by a different mechanism -- a route configuration
// file, or @NoCSRFRequired-style annotations added elsewhere -- is not
// recognised.
binding sessionUserInstallWithoutAuthenticationStateCheck {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "class" and node.context.language == "php"
    and containsAny(node.context.identifier, ["IUserSession"])
    and node.context.callPath contains "userSession.setUser"
    and node.token contains "@PublicPage"
    and not (node.context.call in ["isLoggedIn"])
  emit issue code.SessionUserInstallWithoutAuthenticationStateCheck at node
}
